40x40 torus, 120 sheep, 40 wolves, 400 ticks

wolf_gain = 10  (predation too weak to sustain wolves)
  sheep  min  118  max  183  final  172
  wolves min    0  max   46  final    0

wolf_gain = 20  (standard regime)
  sheep  min  101  max  165  final  141
  wolves min    6  max   46  final    9
  both persist; peak cross-correlation at lag -39

wolf_gain = 40  (stronger predation, sharper cycle)
  sheep  min   36  max  144  final  102
  wolves min   11  max  108  final   57
  both persist; peak cross-correlation at lag -43

  At wolf_gain = 10 the wolves die out and the sheep sit at carrying
  capacity. At 20 both persist in a bounded cycle, and the peak
  cross-correlation lands well away from lag 0 -- the populations are
  out of phase, which is the classic predator-prey signature.

  Raising wolf_gain does not flip the system to extinction so much as
  drive it through ever-tighter bottlenecks. Sweeping further on this
  same world (300 ticks, seed 1) gives minimum populations of:

    wolf_gain    20    40    60   100   160
    min sheep   101    36    24     2     1
    max wolves   46   108   114   249   320

  By 160 both populations are down to single figures and one more bad
  draw ends the run. That is the collapse the design notes anticipated;
  it just arrives as a bottleneck rather than as a clean extinction at
  the tick counts used here.
